 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 21199,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">An Operating System (OS) serves as the backbone of a computer system, managing hardware resources and providing services to application programs. Two of its most critical functions are <b>Process Management</b> and <b>Memory Management</b>. These components work together to ensure optimal performance, resource utilization, and user experience in a multi-tasking environment.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Process Management</span></span></b><br />\n<span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Process Management is the OS&rsquo;s ability to handle the execution of processes &mdash; independent units of execution with their own program counter, registers, and state.<br />\nKey responsibilities include:</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Process Creation and Termination:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> The OS creates processes using system calls, assigns unique IDs, and removes them upon completion.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Scheduling:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> The scheduler determines which process gets CPU time, using algorithms like FCFS, SJF, or Round Robin.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Context Switching:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> The OS saves the state of a running process and loads another, enabling multitasking.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Synchronization and Communication:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Ensures that processes do not interfere with each other and communicate effectively, using mechanisms like semaphores, mutexes, and message queues.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Effective process management ensures fair CPU allocation, reduces idle time, and maximizes throughput.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Memory Management</span></span></b><br />\n<span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Memory Management controls the allocation and deallocation of memory spaces to processes. It protects memory boundaries and optimizes physical memory usage.<br />\nCore functions include:</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Address Binding:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Mapping logical addresses to physical addresses via the Memory Management Unit (MMU).</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Allocation Strategies:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Using techniques like paging and segmentation to allocate memory without fragmentation.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Virtual Memory Management:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Extending physical memory by swapping pages between RAM and secondary storage, allowing execution of large applications.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Page Replacement Policies:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Algorithms like LRU or FIFO are used when pages need to be swapped in/out, affecting system performance.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Memory management is crucial for ensuring that multiple processes can execute simultaneously without conflicts.</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Interaction Between Process and Memory Management</span></span></b><br />\n<span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">The two components are interdependent:</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Process Loading:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> A process must be allocated memory before execution.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Swapping and Context Switching:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> When context switches occur, memory management ensures that the process&rsquo;s data is preserved or swapped appropriately.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Virtual Memory:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Enables more processes to be in the ready queue, supporting multitasking beyond physical memory limits.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list .5in\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri,&quot;sans-serif&quot;\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Thrashing Control:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"> Poor coordination between memory and process management can lead to thrashing, degrading performance.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Their coordination directly impacts CPU utilization, system throughput, and response time.</span></span></span></p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC202J",
   "LEVEL": 3,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2",
    "PLO3",
    "PLO4"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><b>Discuss the roles of Process Management and Memory Management in an Operating System. How do these two components interact to ensure efficient system performance?</b></span></span></p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 106,
   "TYPE": "lq",
   "key": 21199
  }
 ]
